aph","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Roughly 10% of all college students in the United States are left-handed. Most academic institutions, therefore, try to have at least a few left-handed chairs in each classroom. 225 students are about to enter a lecture hall that has 30 left-handed chairs for a lecture. What is the probability that this is not going to be enough; in other words, what is the probability that more than 30 (or at least 31) of the 225 students are left-handed?","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"lxxvN64DRP"}],"key":"sS7lXUvW44"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s think about this situation.","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"i3gg8LQHcB"}],"key":"W3fLFfpuoW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let X be the number of left-handed students (success) out of the 225 students (trials). X is therefore binomial with n = 225 and p = .1. We are asked to find P(X > 30) or P(X ≥ 31).","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"gNWCXaJvjs"}],"key":"txyP50T7pV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clearly, doing this using the binomial distribution is out of the question.","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"WreMKPDxmz"}],"key":"tRVtebjtA7"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"O0wLNNhkO9"}],"key":"cwvbBrLKXd"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Sectionnest**\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":48,"colu